     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose is above the safe maximum.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to **5100 mg/day**. The usual maximum is **2550 mg/day** for immediate-release (often given as 850 mg three times daily) and **2000 mg/day** for extended-release. This order is about twice the ceiling, which raises the risk of severe stomach and bowel side effects and of lactic acidosis. It may be a transcription error. For example, 850 mg three times daily or 1700 mg/day in divided doses would both be standard.\n\n**2. The order has to come from a prescriber.** This system is set up for clinicians to enter medication orders, and you've said you aren't one. Even with the right dose, the order needs to come from the patient's treating clinician (the attending, resident or other licensed prescriber on the medicine service). That keeps the clinical accountability, the documentation and the verification steps intact.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- Ask the patient's prescriber to check what was meant and enter the order themselves.\n- If this came from a handwritten or verbal instruction, a pharmacist on the medicine service can help work out what the intended dose was.\n\nOnce a clinician confirms a dose within the safe range, I can help them enter the order."
      }
     ],
